        {
          "id": "image-1",
          "kind": "image",
          "url": "https://cdn5.telesco.pe/file/OxvxkHWOQQoB5xGYiMrL21KSP4nknRz6hFv92SiWPkiw4ETSvvWftVLiy3Q6XopN2N2NsLYSrXrDoLiBm1aTm853BxkvMeQScSFrNWrKX-lL6jtQfeRZQPuvfv07uTW9X42KyptcdlFNp88Qm6hYHQVbcLcOEOYCDkUK5mTFmcKQiE8LdVxjC_dIdtZOX7SC7zneLzMKunX5np2FrZHCmvfRmgbGcxw_R5FYi0gU5LF8Xkq3M4fDrSGoZXgjRGRsPvLIyMHrHnjU8VQ40PgT3kk5nPWBx7BpRgL9f4lv7fcTX5LlSX0z0O7fjv3ktmXfTpCp9N9B-vxbPk5ra7kMdw.jpg"
        }
      ],
      "hasMediaInOriginal": true,
      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "📌 J.V.P COMMITTEE.\n\n#Unionanditsterritories\n\n🔸️ Dhar commission report created much resentment and led to the appointment of another Linguistic Provinces Committee by the Congress in December, 1948, itself to examine the whole question afresh. \n\n🔸️ It consisted of Jawaharlal Nehru, Vallahbhai Patel and Pattabhi Sitaramayya and hence, was popularly known as JVP Committee.\n\n🔸️ It submitted its report in April, 1949, and formally rejected language as the basis for reorganisation of states.\n\n🔸️ However, in October, 1953, the Government of India was forced to create the first linguistic state, known as Andhra state, by separating the Telugu speaking areas from the Madras state. \n\n🔸️ This followed a prolonged popular agitation and the death of Potti  Sriramulu, a Congress person of standing, after a 56-day hunger strike for the cause.",
